---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AreaLookup from "../components/AreaLookup.astro";
---
<Base
  title="area &amp; route flood-evidence lookup · FloodWatch.PH"
  pathname="/lookup"
  description="Look up dated flood evidence for a place or a route across Greater Metro Manila and the SLEX/NLEX expressways, right in your browser. Each layer is dated. Not a forecast, not safety advice. Your query never leaves your browser."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-8 pb-2">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-2 font-mono">
      the gap between what the model predicts and what past records show, as a tool
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-3 leading-tight text-ink-900">
      Is this place predicted to flood, but barely on record?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Type a place, barangay, landmark, or expressway interchange in Greater
      Metro Manila, or pick two points to test a route. FloodWatch states
      the gap between what the model predicts and what past records show as one
      careful sentence, then shows the dated evidence behind it: what the
      flood-prediction model expects, what past records show, the most recent
      satellite pass, and rainfall and expressway context. It is not a flood
      score and not a verdict. Each row carries its own date. Coverage beyond
      the SLEX/NLEX expressways is a later refinement.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      FloodWatch is independent, open, and anyone can re-run it and get the
      identical result. It works alongside PAGASA, Project NOAH, MMDA Flood
      Control, and Google Flood Hub, and never replaces them. It never
      forecasts. The whole lookup runs in your browser against a place list
      shipped with the site; your query is never sent, logged, or republished.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AreaLookup />

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-6 text-xs text-ink-500 leading-relaxed">
    <p class="mb-2">
      Evidence assembled in your browser from the same public layers the rest of
      the site publishes: the flood-prediction model (Track B; a satellite
      fingerprint of the land from 2017, on a 300 m grid), the Global Flood
      Database record of past floods (2002-2017), the most recent Sentinel-1
      radar (it sees through typhoon clouds) pass (Copernicus, about a 6-day
      gap between passes, about 24 hours before the data is ready, seen by
      satellite not live), recent rainfall totals for context, and the
      OpenStreetMap expressway network overlaid with the latest flood map.
      Place coordinates are public OpenStreetMap / PSA reference points.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-2 italic">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data. Patterns may
      have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification.
      Not an official hazard map. Not a forecast. Not routing or safety advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cite as: FloodWatch.PH (2026),
      <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph</a>.
      CC-BY-4.0 on data.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
